== Chapter XV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[Decays in Degrees of Grace caused by Indwelling Sin. The Ways of its prevalency to this purpose.]
]

#smallcaps[THE] Ways and Means whereby #emph[In-dwelling Sin] prevaileth on Believers unto habitual #emph[declensions] and decaies, as to #emph[degrees of Grace] and Holiness, is that now which comes under consideration, and are many.

#emph[First,] Upon the #emph[first Conversion] and calling of #emph[Sinners] unto God and Christ, they have usually many #emph[fresh Springs] breaking forth in their Souls, and Refreshing showers coming upon them, which bear them up to an #emph[high rate] of Faith, Love, Holiness, Fruitfulness and Obedience. As upon a #emph[Land-flood] when many lesser streams run into a River, it swells over its bounds, and rouls on with a more than ordinary fulness. Now if these #emph[Springs] be not kept open, if they prevail not for the continuance of these showers, they must needs decay and go backwards. We shall name #emph[one or two] of them.

First, They have #emph[a fresh, vigorous sence of Pardoning Mercy.] According as this is in the Soul, so will its Love and delight in God, so will its Obedience be. As, I say, is the sence of Gospel-pardon, so will be the #emph[life] of Gospel-love. #emph[Luke] 7. 47. #emph[I say unto thee,] saith our Saviour of the poor Woman, #emph[her sins, which were many, are forgiven, for she loved much, but to whom little is forgiven, the same loueth little.] Her #emph[great love] was an evidence of #emph[great forgiveness;] and her great sence of it. For our Saviour is not rendering a reason of her #emph[forgiveness,] as though it were for her Love, but of her #emph[Love,] that it was because of her forgiveness. Having in the foregoing #emph[Parable] from #emph[Verse] 38. And onwards, convinced the #emph[Pharisees] with whom he had to do, that he to whom #emph[most] was #emph[forgiven] would love most, as #emph[Verse] 43. He thence gives an account of the #emph[great love] of the Woman springing from the sence she had of the #emph[great forgiveness] which she had so freely received. Thus sinners at their first Conversion are very sensible of great forgiveness: #emph[Of whom I am chief,] lies next their heart. This greatly subdues their hearts and spirits unto all in God, and quickens them unto all Obedience; even that such poor cursed sinners as they were, should so #emph[freely] be delivered and pardoned. The love of God and of Christ in their #emph[forgiveness,] highly conquers and constrains them to make it their business to #emph[live unto God.]

#emph[Secondly,] The #emph[fresh taste they have had of spiritual things,] keeps up such a favour and relish of them in their Souls, as that worldly Contentments whereby men are drawn off from close walking with G•d, are rendered sapless and undesireable unto them. Having tasted of the #emph[Wine] of the Gospel they desire no other, for they say, #emph[this is best.] So was it with the Apostles upon that #emph[option] offered them as to a departure from Christ, upon the Apostasy of many false Professors; #emph[Will ye go away also?] Joh. 6. 67. They answer by #emph[Peter, Lord, to whom shall we go, thou hast the words of eternal life?] Vers. 68. They had such a #emph[fresh savour] and relish of the Doctrine of the Gospel, and the Grace of Christ upon their Souls, that they can entertain no thoughts of declining from it. As a man that hath been long kept in a #emph[dungeon,] if brought forth on a sudden into the Light of the Sun, finds so much pleasure and contentment in it, in the beauties of the old Creation, that he thinks he can never be weary of it, nor shall ever be contented on any account to be under darkness again. So is it with Souls when first transl•ted into the #emph[marvellous Light] of Christ, to behold the beauties of the #emph[New Creation.] They see a new #emph[Glory] in him, that hath quite sullied the desireableness of all earthly diversions. And they see a new guilt and filth in sin, that gives them an utter abhorrency of its old delights and pleasures; and so of other things.

Now whilest these and the #emph[like Springs] are kept open in the Souls of converted sinners, they constrain them to a vigorous active Holiness. They can never #emph[do enough for God;] so that oftentimes their Zeal, as #emph[Saints,] suffers them not to escape without some blots on their Prudence #emph[as men,] as might be instanced in many of the #emph[Martyrs] of old.

This then is the #emph[first,] at least #emph[one way] whereby indwelling-sin prepares men for #emph[decays] and declensions in Grace and Obedience: It endeavours to #emph[stop] or taint #emph[these springs.] And there are several ways whereby it bringeth this to pass.

#emph[First,] It works by #emph[sloth] and #emph[negligence.] It prevails in the Soul to a neglect of #emph[stirring up] continual thoughts of, or about the things that so powerfully #emph[influence] it unto strict and fruitful obedience. If care be not taken, if diligence and watchfulness be not used, and all means observed that are appointed of God, to keep a quick and living sense of them upon the Soul, they will dry up and decay, and consequently that Obedience that should spring from them will do so also. #emph[Isaac digged Wells,] but the #emph[Philistins] stopt them, and his Flocks had no benefit by them. Let the heart never so little di•use it self to gracious soul-affecting thoughts of the Love of God, the Cross of Christ, the greatness and excellency of Gospel mercy, the beauties of holiness, they will quickly be as as much #emph[estranged] to a man, as he can be to them. He that shuts his eyes for a season in the Sun, when he opens them again can see nothing at all. And so much as a man loseth of #emph[faith] towards these things, so much will they lose of #emph[power] towards him. They can do little or nothing upon him, because of his unbelief, which formerly were so exceedingly effectual towards him. So was it with the #emph[Spouse] in the #emph[Canticles,] chap. 5. 2. Christ calls unto her, #emph[ver.] 1. With a marvellous #emph[loving] and gracious invitation unto communion with himself. She who had formerly been #emph[ravished] at the first hearing of that #emph[joyful sound,] being now under the power of #emph[sloth] and carnal ease, returns a sorry #emph[excusing] answer to his Call, which ended in her own signal loss and sorrow. #emph[Indwelling-sin,] I say, prevailing by #emph[spiritual sloth] upon the souls of men unto an inadvertency of the motions of Gods Spirit in their former apprehensions of divine Love, and a negligence of stirring up continual thoughts of #emph[faith] about it, a #emph[decay] grows insensibly upon the whole soul. Thus God oft complains that his people had #emph[forgotten him,] that is, grew unmindful of his Love and Grace, which was the beginning of their #emph[Apostasy.]

Secondly, By #emph[unframing the soul,] so that it shall have formal, weary, #emph[powerless] thoughts of those things, which should prevail with it unto #emph[diligence] in thankful obedience. The Apostle cautions us, that in dealing with God, we should #emph[use reverence and godly fear,] because of his purity, holiness, and Majesty, #emph[Heb.] 12. 28, 29. And this is that which the Lord himself spake in the destruction of #emph[Nadab] and #emph[Abihu, I will be sanctified in them that come to me,] Lev. 10. 3. He will be dealt withal •in an awful, #emph[holy,] reverent manner. So are we to deal with all the things of God, wherein, or whereby we have communion with him. The soul is to have a great Reverence of God in them. When men begin to take them into #emph[slight] and #emph[common thoughts,] not using and improving them unto the utmost for the ends whereunto they are appointed, they lose all their beauty, and glory, and power towards them. When we have any thing to do wherein faith and love towards God is to be exercised, we must do it with all our hearts, with all our minds, strength and souls, not slightly and #emph[perfunctorily,] which God abhors; he doth not only require that we bear his Love and Grace in remembrance, but that, as much as in us lyeth, we do it according to the worth and excellency of them. It was the sin of #emph[Hezekiah,] that he #emph[rendered not again according to the benefits done to him,] 2 Chron. 32. 25. So whilst we consider Gospel truths, the uttermost endeavour of the soul ought to be, that we may be changed into the same image or likeness, 2 #emph[Cor.] 3. 18. That is, that they may have their full power and effect upon us. Otherwise #emph[James] tells us what our #emph[beholding the glory of the Lord in a glass,] there mentioned by the Apostle, that is, reading or hearing the mind of God in Christ revealed in the Gospel, comes unto, #emph[chap.] 1. #emph[vers.] 23, 24. #emph[It is but like unto a man beholding his natural face in a glass, for he beholdeth himself and goeth away, and streight way forgeteth what manner of man he was.] It makes no impression upon him, begets no #emph[Idea] nor image of his likeness in his imagination, because he doth it only #emph[slightly,] and with a transient look. So is it with men that will indeed think of #emph[Gospel truths,] but in a slight manner, without endeavouring with all their hearts, minds, and strength, to have them ingrafted upon their souls, and all the effects of them produced in them. Now this is the way of #emph[sinners,] in their first #emph[engagements] unto God. They never think of #emph[pardoning mercy,] but they labour to affect their whole souls with it, and do stir up themselves unto suitable affections and returns of constant obedience. They think not of the #emph[Excellency of Christ,] and spiritual things, now newly discovered unto them in a saving light, but they press with all their might after a farther, a fuller enjoyment of them. This keeps them #emph[humble] and holy, this makes them thankful and fruitful. But now if the utmost #emph[diligence] and carefulness be not used to improve and grow in this Wisdom, to keep up this frame, #emph[indwelling-sin] working by the #emph[vanity of the minds of men;] will insensibly bring them to content themselves with #emph[slight] and #emph[rare] thoughts of these things, without a diligent sedulous endeavour to give them their due improvement upon the soul. As men #emph[decay] herein, so they will assuredly #emph[decay] and decline in the #emph[power of holiness,] and close walking with God. The #emph[springs] being stopt or tainted, the streams will not run so #emph[swiftly,] at least not so #emph[sweetly] as formerly. Some by this means under an uninterrupted profession, insensibly wither almost into nothing. They talk of Religion and spiritual things as much as ever they did in their lives, and perform #emph[duties] with as much constancy as ever they did, but yet have poor lean starvling souls, as to any real and effectual communion with God. By the power and subtilty of #emph[indwelling-sin] they have grown #emph[formal,] and learned to deal about spiritual things in an #emph[overly] manner, whereby they have lost all their life, vigour, favour, and efficacy towards them. Be always #emph[serious] in spiritual things, if ever you intend to be bettered by them.

#emph[Thirdly, Indwelling-sin] oftentimes prevails to the stopping of these springs of Gospel obedience, by false and foolish opinions, corrupting the #emph[simplicity] of the Gospel. False opinions are the #emph[works of the flesh.] From the #emph[vanity] and darkness of the minds of men, with a mixture more or less of corrupt affections, do they mostly proceed. The Apostle was #emph[jealous] over his #emph[Corinthians] in this matter, he was afraid lest their minds should #emph[by any means be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ,] 2 Cor. 11. 2, 3. Which he knew would be attended by a #emph[decay] and declension in faith, love, and obedience. And thus matters in this case often fall out. We have seen some, who after they have received a sweet #emph[taste] of the love of God in Christ, of the excellency of #emph[pardoning mercy,] and have walked humbly with God for many years, in the faith and apprehension of the truth, have by the corruption of their minds from the simplicity that is in Christ by false and foolish opinions, despised all their own experiences, and rejected all the efficacy of truth, as to the fartherance of their obedience. Hence #emph[John] cautions the #emph[Elect Lady] and her children to take heed they were not seduced, #emph[lest they should lose the things that they had wrought,] 2 Epist. v. 8. Lest they should themselves cast away all their former obedience as lost, and a thing of no value. We have innumerable instances hereof in the days wherein we live. How many are there, who not many years since put an unspeakable value #emph[on the pardon of sin in the blood of Christ,] who delighted in Gospel discoveries of spiritual things, and walked in obedience unto God on the account of them, who being #emph[beguiled] and turned aside from the truth as it is in Jesus, do despise these #emph[springs] of their own former obedience? And as this is done #emph[grosly] and openly in some, so there are more #emph[secret] and more #emph[plausible] insinuations of corrupt opinions, tainting the springs and fountains of Gospel obedience, and through the vanity of mens minds, which is a principal part of indwelling-sin, getting ground upon them. Such are all those that tend to the extenuation of #emph[special Grace] in its freedom, and efficacy, and the advancement of the wills or the endeavours of men in their spiritual power and ability: They are all #emph[works of the flesh,] and howsoever some may pretend an usefulness in them to the #emph[promotion of holiness,] they will be found to #emph[taint the springs] of true Evangelical obedience, insensibly to turn the heart from God, and to bring the whole soul into a spiritual #emph[decay.]

And this is one way whereby #emph[Indwelling-sin] produceth this pernicious effect, of drawing men off from the power, purity, and fruitfulness attending their first conversion, and engagements unto God, bringing them into #emph[habitual declension,] at least as unto #emph[degrees] of their holiness and grace. There is not any thing we ought to be more watchful against, if we intend effectually to deal with this powerful and subtile enemy. It is no small part of the #emph[wisdom of faith,] to observe whether Gospel truths continue to have the same #emph[savour] unto, and #emph[efficacy] upon the soul, as formerly they have had; and whether an indeavour be maintained to improve them continually as at the first. A commandment that is always practised, is always new, as #emph[John] speaks of that of Love. And he that really improves Gospel truths, though he hears them a thousand times, they will be always #emph[new] and fresh unto him, because they put him on #emph[newness] of practice. When to another that grows #emph[common] under them, they are burthensome and #emph[common] unto him; and he even loaths the #emph[Manna] that he is so accustomed unto.

#emph[Secondly, Indwelling-sin] doth this, #emph[by taking men off from their watch against the returns of Satan.] When our Lord Christ comes first to take possession of any soul for himself, he binds that #emph[strong man and spoils his goods;] he deprives them of all his power, dominion and interest. Satan being thus dispossest and frustrated in his hopes and expectations, leaves the soul, as finding it newly mortified to his baits. So he left our Saviour upon his first fruitless attempts. But it is said, #emph[He left him only for a season,] Luk. 4. 13. He intended to return again, as he should see his advantage. So is it with #emph[Believers] also. Being cast out from his interest in them, he leaves them for a #emph[season,] at least comparatively he doth so. Freed from his assaults and perplexing temptations, they proceed #emph[vigorously] in the course of their obedience, and so flourish in the ways of God. But this holds not; Satan returns again, and if the soul stands not continually upon his #emph[guard] against him, he will quickly get such advantages, as shall put a notable interruption upon his fruitfulness and obedience. Hence some, after they have spent #emph[some time,] it may be #emph[some years] in cheerful exemplary walking with God, have upon Satans #emph[return,] consumed all their latter days in wrestling with perplexing temptations, wherewith he hath intangled them. Others have plainly fallen under the power of his assaults. It is like a man, who having for a while lived #emph[usefully] amongst his neighbours, done good, and communicated according to his ability, distributing to the poor, and helping all round about him, at length falling into the hands of vexatious, wrangling oppressive men, he is forced to spend his whole time and revenue in defending himself against them at Law, and so becomes useless in the place where he lives. So is it with many a Believer; after he hath walked in a fruitful course of obedience to the glory of God, and edification of the Church of Christ, being afresh set upon by the #emph[ret•rn] of Satan in one way or other, he hath enough to do all the remainder of his life to keep himself #emph[a•ive;] in the mean time a to many graces, wofully decaying and going backward. Now this also, though Satan hath an hand in it, is from #emph[Indwelling-sin:] I mean, the success is so which Satan doth obtain in his undertaking. This encourageth him, maketh way for his return, and gives entrance to his temptations. You know how it is with them, out of whom he is cast only by #emph[Gospel conviction;] after he hath wandred and waited awhile, he saith, #emph[He will return to his house from whence he was ejected.] And what is the issue? Carnal lusts have prevailed over the mans convictions, and made his soul fit to entertain #emph[returning devils.] It is so as to the measure of prevalency, that Satan obtains against Believers, upon advantages administered unto him, by sins disposing the soul unto an obnoxiousness to his temptations.

Now the way and means whereby #emph[Indwelling-sin] doth give advantage to Satan for his #emph[return,] are all those which dispose them towards a #emph[declension] which shall afterwards be mentioned. Satan is a diligent, watchful, and crafty adversary, he will neglect no opportunity, no advantage that is offered unto him. Wherein then soever our spiritual strength is impaired by #emph[sin,] or which way soever our lusts press, Satan falls in with that weakness, and presseth towards that ruin. So that all the actings of the Law of sin are subservient to this end of Satan. I shall therefore only at present mention one or two, that seem princicipally to invite Satan to attempt a #emph[return.]

#emph[First,] It intangleth the soul in the things of the world, all which are so many Purveyors for Satan. When #emph[Pharaoh] had let the people go, he heard after a while that they were #emph[intangled in the wilderness] and supposeth that he shall therefore now #emph[overtake] them and destroy them. This stirs him up to #emph[pursue] after them. Satan finding those whom he hath been cast out from, intangled in the things of the world, by which he is sure to find an easy #emph[access] unto them, is encouraged to attempt upon them afresh; as the #emph[Spider] to come down upon the strongest fly that is #emph[entangled] in his web. For he comes by his temptations only to impel them unto that whereunto by their own lusts they are enclined, by adding #emph[poison] to their lusts, and #emph[painting] to the #emph[objects] of them. And oftentimes by this advantage he gets so in upon the souls of men, that they are never well free of him more whilst they live. And as mens #emph[diversions] increase from the world, so do their #emph[entanglements] from Satan. When they have more to do in the world than they can well manage, they shall have more to do from Satan than they can well withstand. When men are made spiritually #emph[faint,] by dealing in and with the world, Satan sets on them a• #emph[Amalek] did on the faint and weak of the people that came out of #emph[Egypt.]

Secondly, It produceth this effect by #emph[making the soul negligent,] and taking it off from its watch. We have before shew'd at large that it is one main part of the #emph[effectual deceitfulness of Indwelling sin,] to make the soul #emph[inadvertent,] to turn it off from the diligent watchful attendance unto its duty, which is required. Now there is not any thing in reference whereunto diligence and watchfulness is more strictly enjoyned; than the #emph[returning assau•ts] of Satan, 1 #emph[Pet.] 5. 8. #emph[Be sober, be vigilant;] and why so? #emph[because of your adversary the Devil.] Unless you are exceeding #emph[watchful,] at one time or other he will surprize you. And all the injunctions of our blessed #emph[Saviour,] to #emph[watch,] are still with reference unto him, and his temptations. Now when the soul is made #emph[careless] and inadvertent, forgetting what an enemy it hath to deal withal, or is lifted up with the #emph[successes] it hath newly obtained against him, then in Satans time to attempt a re-entrance of his old #emph[habitation;] which if he cannot obtain, yet he makes their lives uncomfortable to themselves, and unfruitful to others, in weakening their #emph[root,] and withering their #emph[fruit] through his poisoning temptations. He comes down upon our duties of #emph[obedience,] as the fowls upon #emph[Abrahams] sacrifice, that if we watch not, as he did, to drive them away, \[for by resistance he is overcome and put to flight\] he will #emph[devour] them.

Thirdly, #emph[Indwelling-sin] takes advantage to put forth its efficacy and deceit, to with-draw men from their #emph[primitive zeal] and holiness, from their #emph[first faith, love,] and works by the #emph[evil examples of Professors] amongst whom they live. When men first engage into the ways of God, they have a #emph[reverent esteem] of those whom they believe to have been made partakers of that mercy before themselves; these they love and honour, as it is their duty. But after a while they find many of them walking in many things #emph[unevenly, crookedly,] & not unlike the men of the world. Here #emph[sin] is not wanting to its advantage. Insensibly it prevails with men to a compliance with them. This way, this course of walking, doth #emph[well enough] with others, why may it not do so with us also? Such is the inward thought of many, that works effectually in them. And so through the craft of #emph[sin,] the generation of Professors corrupt one another. As a #emph[stream] arising from a clear spring or fountain, whilst it runs in its own peculiar channel, and keeps its water unmixed, preserves its purity and cleanness, but when it falls in its course with other streams that are #emph[turbid] and #emph[foul,] though running the same way with it, it becomes #emph[muddy] and #emph[discoloured] also. So is it in this case. Believers come forth from the spring of the #emph[New birth] with some purity and cleanness, this for a while they keep in the course of their private walking with God; but now when they come sometimes to fall into #emph[society] with #emph[others,] whose profession flows & runs the same way with theirs, even towards heaven, but yet are #emph[mudded] and #emph[sullied] with sin and the world, they are often corrupted with them, and by them, and so decline from their first purity, faith, and holiness. Now lest this may have been the case of any who shall read this discourse, I shall add some few cautions that are #emph[necessary] to preserve men from this infection.

#emph[First,] In the #emph[body of Professors] there is a great number of #emph[hypocrites.] Though we cannot say of #emph[this] or #emph[that] man, that he is so, yet that some there are, is most certain. Our Saviour hath told us that it will be so to the end of the world. All that have #emph[Oyl] in their #emph[Lamps,] have it not in their #emph[vessels.] Let men take heed how they give themselves up unto a #emph[conformity] to the Professors they meet withal, lest instead of #emph[Saints] and the best of men, they sometimes propose for their example #emph[hypocrites,] which are the #emph[worst,] and when they think they are #emph[like] unto them who bare the #emph[image of God,] they conform themselves to those, who bare the #emph[Image of Satan.]

#emph[Secondly,] You know not what may be the #emph[present temptations] of those whose #emph[ways] you observe. It may be they are under some #emph[peculiar desertion] from God, and so are #emph[withering] for a season, until he send them some #emph[refreshing powers] from above. It may be they are intangled with some #emph[special corruptions,] which is their burthen, tat you know not of; and for any voluntarily to fall into such a frame, as others are cast into by the power of their temptations, or to think that will suffice in them, which they see to suffice in others whose distempers they know not, is #emph[folly] and presumption. He that knows #emph[such] or #emph[such] a person to be a living Man, and of an healthy constitution, if he see him go #emph[crawling] up and down about his affairs, feeble and weak, sometimes falling, sometimes standing, and making small progress in any thing, will he think it sufficient for himself to do so also? Will he not inquire whether the person he sees, have not lately fallen into some #emph[distemper] or #emph[sickness,] that hath weakened him, and brought him into that condition? Assuredly he will so do. Take heed, Christians, many of the Professors with whom ye do converse are sick, and wounded; the wounds of some of them do #emph[stink, and are corrupt because of their folly.] If you have any #emph[spiritual health,] do not think their weak and uneven walking will be accepted at your hands; much less ••ink it will be well for you to become #emph[sick] and to be wounded also.

#emph[Thirdly,] Remember that of many of the #emph[best Christians,] the #emph[worst] only is known and seen. Many who keep up precious Communion with God, do yet oftentimes by their #emph[natural temper•] of freedom or passion, not carry so glorious appearances as others, who perhaps come short of them in Grace and the power of Godliness. In respect of their outward Conversation it may seem they are scarcely #emph[saved,] when in respect of their #emph[Faith and Love] they may be eminent. They may, as the #emph[Kings Daughter,] be all glorious within, though their #emph[Cloaths be not always of wrought Gold.] Take heed then that you be not infected with their #emph[worst,] when ye are not able it may be to imitate them in their #emph[best;] but to return.

#emph[Fourthly,] Sin doth this work by #emph[cherishing some secret particular lust in the heart.] This the soul contends against faintly, It #emph[contends against] it upon the account of sincerity, it cannot but do so; but it doth not make through-work vigorously to mortify it by the strength and power of Grace. Now where it is thus with the Soul, an #emph[habitual dec•ension] as to holiness will assuredly ensue. #emph[David] shows us how in his #emph[first days] he kept his heart close unto God, #emph[Psal] 18. 23. #emph[I was upright before him, 〈◊〉 I kept my self from mine iniquity.] His great care was lest any #emph[one lust] should prevail in him, or upon him, that might be called #emph[his iniquity] in s• #emph[peculiar manner.] The same course steer'd #emph[Paul] also • #emph[Cor.] 9. 27▪ he was in #emph[danger to be lifted up] by his spiritua••evelations and Enjoyments. This makes him keep #emph[his body in subjection,] that no carnal reasonings or vain imagination might take place in him. But where #emph[indwelling Sin] hath provoked, irritated, and given strength unto a #emph[special lust,] it proves assuredly a #emph[Principal Means] of a general declension. For as an infirmity and weakness in any one vital part, will make the whole body consumptive, so will the #emph[weakness in any one Grace,] which a perplexing lust brings with it, make the Soul. It every way weakens spiritual strength. It weakens #emph[Confidence] in God in Faith and Prayer. The #emph[knees] will be feeble, and the #emph[hands] will hang down in dealing with God, where a galling and unmortified lust lies in the heart. It will take #emph[such hold upon the soul, that it shall not be able to look up,] Psal. 40. 12. It darkens the mind by #emph[innumerable foolish imaginations,] which it stirs up to make provision for its self. It #emph[galls] the Conscience with those spots and stains, which in and by its actings it brings upon the soul. It contends in the #emph[Will] for Rule and Dominion: An #emph[active stirring corruption] would have the commanding power in the soul, and it is ever and anon ready to take the Throne. It #emph[disturbs] the thoughts, and sometimes will even frighten the soul from dealing with it by meditation, lest corrupt affections being entangled by it, Grace loses ground instead of prevailing. It #emph[breaks] out oftentimes into scandalous sins, as it did in #emph[David] and #emph[Hezekiah,] and loads the sinner with sorrow and discouragemen. By these and the like means it becomes to the soul like a #emph[moth in a garment,] to eat up and devour the strongest threeds of it, so that though the whole hang #emph[loose] together, it is easily torn in pieces. Though the soul with whom it is thus, do for a season keep up a #emph[fair profession,] yet his strength is secretly devoured; and every temptation tears and rents his #emph[Conscience] at pleasure. It becomes with such Men as it is with some who have for a many years been of a sound strong #emph[athletick] constitution. Some secret #emph[hectical distemper] seizeth on them: For a season they take no notice of it; or if they do, they think they shall do well enough with it, and easily shake it off when they have a little leasure to attend unto it: But for the present they think as #emph[Samson] with his locks cut, they will do as at other times. Sometimes it may be they complain that they are not #emph[well,] they know not what aileth them, and it may be rise violently in an opposition to their distemper; but after a while #emph[strugling] in vain, the vigour of their spirits and strength failing them, they are forced to yield to the power of a #emph[consumption.] And now all they can do is little enough to keep them alive. It is so with Men brought into #emph[spiritual decays] by any secret perplexing corruption. It may be they have had a vigorous principle of obedience and holiness: #emph[Indwelling Sin] watching its opportunities, by some temptation or other, hath kindled and inflamed some particular lust in them. For a while it may be they take little notice of it. Somtimes they #emph[complain,] but think they will do as in former times, until being insensibly weakned in their spiritual strength, they hav• work enough to do in keeping alive what remains and is ready to dye, #emph[Hos.] 5. 13. I shall not add any thing here as to the prevention and obviating this advantage of Indwelling-sin, having elsewhere treated of it peculiarly and apart.

#emph[Fifthly,] It works by #emph[negligence of private conmunion with God in Prayer and Meditation.] I have showed before how #emph[Indwelling Sin] puts forth its #emph[deceitfulness] in diverting the soul from watchfulness in and unto these duties. Here if it prevails, it will not fail to induce an #emph[habitual declension] in the whole course of Obedience. All neglect of private duties is principled by a #emph[weariness] of God as he complaineth, #emph[Isa.] 43. 22. #emph[Thou hast not called upon Me, thou hast been weary of Me.] Neglect of invocation proceeds from weariness. And where there is #emph[weariness,] there will be withdrawing from that whereof we are weary. Now God alone being the fountain and spring of spiritual life, if there be a #emph[weariness] of him, and withdrawing from him, it is impossible but that there will #emph[a decay] in the Life ensue. Indeed what men are in #emph[these• duties,] I mean as to Faith and Love in them, that they are and no more. Here lies the root of their obedience, and if this fail all fruit will quickly fail. You may sometimes see a #emph[Tree] flourishing with leaves and fruit goodly and pleasant. After a while the #emph[leaves] begin to decay, the #emph[fruit] to wither, the whole to droop. Search, and you shall find the #emph[root,] whereby it should draw in moisture and fatness from the Earth to supply the body and branches with sap and juice for growth and fruit, hath received a wound, is some way perished, and doth not perform its duty, so that though the branches are flourishing a while with what they had received their sustenance being intercepted they must decay. So it is here. These duties of private Communion with God, are the means of receiving supplies of #emph[spiritual strength] from him; of sap & fatness from Christ the Vine and Olive. Whilest they do so, the conversation and course of obedience #emph[flourisheth] and is fruitful, all outward duties are chearfully and regularly performed. But if there be a #emph[wound,] a defect, a failing in that which should first take in the #emph[spiritual radical moisture,] that should be communicated unto the whole, the rest may for a season maintain their station and appearance, but after a while profession will wither, fruits will decay, and the whole be ready to die. Hence our Saviour lets us know, #emph[Matth.] 6. 6. What a man is #emph[in secret,] in these private duties, that he is in the eyes of God, and no more. And one reason amongst others is, because they have a more vigorous acting of #emph[unmixed grace] than any other duties whatever. In all or most particular duties, besides the influence that they may have from carnal respects, which are many, and the ways of their insinuation subtile, and imperceptible, there is an #emph[allay] of #emph[gifts,] which sometime even devours the pure Gold of Grace which should be the chief and Principal in them. In these, there is immediate entercourse between God, and that which is of himself in the soul. If once sin by its deceits and treacheries, prevail to take off the Soul from #emph[diligent attendance] unto Communion with God, and constancy in these duties, it will not fail to effect a #emph[declining] in the whole of a mans obedience. It hath made its entrance, and will assuredly make good its progress.

#emph[Sixthly,] Growing in #emph[Notions of Truth without answerable practice,] is another thing that #emph[Indwelling-sin] makes use to bring the souls of Believers under a #emph[decay.] The Apostle tells us, that #emph[knowledge puffeth up,] 1 #emph[Cor.] 8. 1. If it be alone, not improved in practice, it #emph[swells men] beyond a due proportion. Like a man that hath a #emph[Dropsy,] we are not to expect that he hath strength to his bigness. Like trees that are continually #emph[running up an head,] which keeps them from bearing fruit. When once men have attained to this, that they can entertain and receive #emph[Evangelical truths] in a new and more Glorious light, or more clear discovery than formerly, or new manifestations of Truth which they knew not before, and please themselves in so doing, without #emph[diligent endeavors] to have the power of those truths and notions upon their hearts and their souls made conformable unto them, they generally learn so to dispose of all truths formerly known, which were sometimes inlaid in their hearts with more efficacy and power. This hath proved, if not the #emph[ruin,] yet the great #emph[impairing] of many in these #emph[days of light] wherein we live. By this means from humble close #emph[walking,] many have withered into an empty, barren, #emph[talking] profession. All things almost have in a short season become alike unto them: Have they been true or false, so they might be debating of them, and disputing about them, all is well. This is #emph[food for sin,] it hatcheth, increaseth it, and is increased by it. A notable way it is for the #emph[vanity] that is in the mind, to exert it self without a rebuke from conscience. Whilst men are talking, and writing, and studying about Religion, and hearing #emph[Preaching,] it may be, with great #emph[delight,] as those in #emph[Ezekiel chap.] 33. 32. Conscience. Unless thoroughly awake and circumspect, and furnished with spiritual wisdom and care, will be very well pacified, and enter no rebukes or pleas against the way that the soul is in. But yet all this may be nothing but the acting of that #emph[natural vanity] which lies in the mind, and is a principal part of the sin we treat of. And generally this is so, when men content themselves, as was said, with the notions of truth, without labouring after an experience of the power of them in their hearts, and the bringing forth the fruit of them in their lives, on which a #emph[decay] must needs ensue.

#emph[Seventhly, Growth in carnal wisdom] is another help to sin in producing this sad effect. #emph[Thy wisdom and thy knowledge,] saith the Prophet, #emph[hath perverted thee, Isa.] 47. 10. So much as #emph[carnal wisdom] increaseth, so much faith decays. The proper work of it is to teach a man #emph[to trust to and in himself,] of faith to trust wholly in another. So it labours to destroy the whole work of faith, by causing the soul to return into a deceiving #emph[fulness] of his own. We have woful examples of the prevalency of this principle of declension in the days werein we live. How many a poor, humble, broken hearted creature, who followed after God in simplicity and integrity of spirit, have we seen, through the observation of the ways and walkings of others, and closing with the temptations to craft and subtilty, which opportunities in the world have administered unto them, come to be dipt in a #emph[worldly carnal frame,] and utterly to wither in their profession. Many are so #emph[sullied] hereby, that they are not to be known to be the men they were.

#emph[Eightly, Some great sin lying long in the heart and conscience unrepented of,] or not repented of as it ought, and as the matter requires, furthers #emph[indwelling-sin] in this work. The great turn of the life of #emph[David,] whence his #emph[first ways] carried the reputation, was in the harbouring his great sin in his conscience without suitable repentance. It was otherwise we know with #emph[Peter,] and he had another issue. A great sin will certainly give a great #emph[turn] to the life of a Professor. If it be well cured in the blood of Christ, with that humiliation which the Gospel requires, it often proves a means of more watchfulness, fruitfulness, humility, and contentation, than ever the soul before obtained. If it be neglected, it certainly #emph[hardens the heart,] weakens spiritual strength, enfeebles the soul, discouraging it unto all communion with God, and is a notable principle of a general decay. So #emph[David] complains, #emph[Ps.] 38. 5. #emph[My wounds stink and are corrupt because of my foolishness.] His present distemper was not so much from his #emph[sin,] as his #emph[folly,] not so much from the wounds he had received, as from his neglect to make a timely application for their cure. It is like a #emph[broken bone,] which being well set, leaves the place stronger than before; if otherwise, makes the man a #emph[Cripple] all his days. These things we do but briefly name, and sundry other advantages of the like nature that #emph[sin] makes use of to produce this effect, might also be instanced in; but these may suffice unto our present purpose. What ever it useth, it self is still the principle; and this is no small demonstration of its efficacy and power.
